---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
    title="Install · Sanctum Family Pass"
    description="The six-step install for Sanctum Family Pass v1.0. Hash-pinned, two-step, no surprises."
>
    <main>
        <p class="tagline">Install · v1.0 pilot</p>

        <h1>Install Sanctum, six steps</h1>

        <p class="lede">
            One curl pair, one hash check, one bash run. The whole install
            is in front of you on disk before anything runs — that is the
            point of the two-step pattern. If you came expecting a
            thousand-line bootstrap script with a fugue-state config
            phase, you have the wrong era of Sanctum.
        </p>

        <h2>Before you start</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Mac with Apple Silicon, macOS 14 or newer</li>
            <li>16 GB RAM minimum, 80 GB free disk</li>
            <li>Firewalla on your LAN (you do not need to pair it yet)</li>
            <li>Homebrew installed</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The installer handles the rest — Lima, socket_vmnet, age,
            sops, and the LaunchAgent stack. If something is missing it
            tells you what is missing and stops. It does not silently
            install Xcode for you.
        </p>

        <h2>The six steps</h2>

        <h3>1. Download the installer and its hash</h3>
        <pre><code>{`curl -fsSLO https://sanctum.haus/install.sh
curl -fsSLO https://sanctum.haus/install.sh.sha256`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            Two files. The script, and the cryptographic fingerprint of
            the script. The fingerprint is short. The script is not.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Verify the hash</h3>
        <pre><code>{`shasum -a 256 -c install.sh.sha256`}</code></pre>
        <p>Expected output:</p>
        <pre><code>{`install.sh: OK`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            If it does <em>not</em> say <code>OK</code>, stop. The
            download is corrupt, mid-flight tampered, or pointed at the
            wrong file. Re-download both. If the second attempt fails the
            same way, ask in the pilot Signal group before proceeding.
            We did not write this step for fun.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Eyeball the script</h3>
        <pre><code>{`less install.sh`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            The script opens with a numbered plan of what it will do —
            directory creation, Lima VM provisioning, LaunchAgent
            installation, self-test. Read the plan. Then trust it, because
            you read it.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Dry-run first</h3>
        <pre><code>{`bash install.sh --dry-run`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            This walks the plan without making changes. It prints what it
            would create, where, and which sudo prompts you should
            expect. If the dry-run says something you do not want, the
            wet-run will do that same thing.
        </p>

        <h3>5. Run it</h3>
        <pre><code>{`bash install.sh`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            One sudo password prompt — Lima and socket_vmnet need it for
            host networking. The script ends when <code>sanctum
            self-test</code> passes. If self-test fails, the installer
            leaves you with a populated <code>~/.sanctum</code> and a
            non-zero exit code;
            <code>sanctum doctor</code> tells you what broke.
        </p>

        <h3>6. Back up your age key</h3>
        <pre><code>{`sanctum keys backup ~/Desktop/sanctum-keys.age`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            Move the resulting file somewhere durable — a USB key, an
            encrypted external disk, wherever you keep recovery codes.
            Without it, your encrypted Sanctum secrets are unrecoverable
            if the Mac fails. There is no recovery email. There is no
            support line that will undo this for you. This is the price
            of running on hardware you actually own.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <h2>After install</h2>
        <p>A short tour of the operator surface:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>sanctum status</code> — daily green/red glance,
                six lines.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>sanctum doctor</code> — full diagnostic, prints
                what failed and a hint per failure.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>sanctum logs &lt;service&gt;</code> — tail any
                subsystem (e.g. <code>sanctum logs firewalla</code>).
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>sanctum self-test</code> — re-run the eleven
                post-install probes any time.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Next step: pair the Firewalla so Sanctum can actually enforce
            schedules. The full pairing walkthrough is in
            <a href="https://www.sanctum.run/getting-started/firewalla-pairing/"
                >the docs</a
            >.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <h2>About this download</h2>
        <p class="muted">
            The <code>install.sh</code> served from this page is the
            current tested HEAD of the
            <code>sanctum-cli</code> repo. For v1.0 pilot we ship from
            HEAD; v1.1 will pin to a signed git tag and rebuild the
            served artifact only on tag push. If you want to verify the
            source, the upstream repo is at
            <code>github.com/Ogilthorp3/sanctum-cli</code> and the
            corresponding <code>install.sh</code> is the file with the
            same SHA-256 as the one you downloaded.
        </p>
    </main>
</Base>
